      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 American Society for Reproductive Medicine. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate a forecasting method developed to predict first trimester pregnancy outcomes using the first routine ultrasound scan for early pregnancy on days 27-29 after ET and to determine whether to perform a repeated scan several days later based on this forecasting method."
          },
          {
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Prospective analysis."
          },
          {
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Infertile patients at an assisted reproductive technology center."
          },
          {
            "Label": "PATIENT(S)",
            "content": "A total of 9,963 patients with an early singleton pregnancy after in vitro fertilization (IVF)-ET."
          },
          {
            "Label": "INTERVENTION(S)",
            "content": "None."
          },
          {
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)",
            "content": "Ongoing pregnancy >12 weeks of gestation."
          },
          {
            "Label": "RESULT(S)",
            "content": "The classification score of ongoing pregnancy was equal to (1.57 × Maternal age) + (1.01 × Mean sac diameter) + (-0.19 × Crown-rump length) + 25.15 (if cardiac activity is present) + 1.30 (if intrauterine hematomas are present) - 47.35. The classification score of early pregnancy loss was equal to (1.66 × Maternal age) + (0.84 × Mean sac diameter) + (-0.38 × Crown-rump length) + 8.69 (if cardiac activity is present) + 1.60 (if intrauterine hematomas are present) - 34.77. In verification samples, 94.44% of cases were correctly classified using these forecasting models."
          },
          {
            "Label": "CONCLUSION(S)",
            "content": "The discriminant forecasting models are accurate in predicting first trimester pregnancy outcomes based on the first scan for early pregnancy after ET. When the predictive result is ongoing pregnancy, a second scan can be postponed until 11-14 weeks if no symptoms of abdominal pain or vaginal bleeding are present. When the predictive results suggest early pregnancy loss, repeated scans are imperative to avoid a misdiagnosis before evacuating the uterus."
          }
        ]
      },
